              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "In Massachusetts, the maximum amount you can collect for unemployment benefits depends on whether you are looking at the weekly limit or the total amount over the life of your claim. \n\nThe breakdown of the current maximum benefits as of **2026** is as follows:\n\n### 1. Maximum Weekly Benefit Amount: **$1,105**\nThe maximum base weekly benefit is **$1,105**. This rate went into effect on October 5, 2025, and remains the active cap for 2026. In Massachusetts, your weekly benefit is generally calculated to be about 50% of your average weekly wage (using your highest-paid quarters), capped at this limit. \n\n### 2. Dependency Allowance (Additional Weekly Funds)\nIf you have qualifying dependents (such as children under the age of 18, full-time students under 24, or disabled adult children) and you are their primary source of support, you can receive an additional **$25 per dependent, per week**. \n* This dependency allowance is **added on top** of the $1,105 base cap. \n* The total dependency allowance you can receive is capped at **50% of your base weekly benefit rate**.\n\n### 3. Maximum Duration of Benefits: **30 Weeks**\nWhile the standard limit in Massachusetts is typically 26 weeks, a state law was triggered in April 2025 due to regional unemployment rates rising above 5.1%. This extended the maximum statewide duration of benefits to **30 weeks**. \n\n### 4. Maximum Total Benefit per Claim Year\nIf you qualify for the maximum weekly base benefit of $1,105 and collect it for the maximum duration of 30 weeks, your maximum total payout for the benefit year is **$33,150** (excluding any dependency allowances). \n\n*Note: To receive the maximum duration and weekly amount, your base period earnings (wages earned during the last year before you filed your claim) must be high enough to support those maximums.*",
